Q: Overlaying a text box on a leaflet.js map A: <p>I know this is old, but here's some sample code, CSS as necessary:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const TextBox = L.Control.extend({
  options: {
    innerHTML: ``,
  },
  onAdd: function () {
    const textbox = L.DomUtil.create(
      `div`,  // tag name
      `info-text`, // CSS class
    );
    textbox.innerHTML = this.options.innerHTML;
    return textbox;
  },
  updateInnerHTML: function (html = ``) {
    this.getContainer().innerHTML = html;
  },
});

const myTextBox = new TextBox({
  innerHTML: `&lt;strong&gt;your text&lt;/strong&gt;`,
  position: `bottomleft`,
})

</code></pre>
<p>And then if you need to change the text later on,</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>myTextBox.updateInnerHTML(`&lt;em&gt;new text here&lt;/em&gt;`);
</code></pre>

Q: How do I keep content from overflowing a flexbox with absolute positioning and 100% height? A: <p>If you have:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div class='main'&gt;
    &lt;div class='title'&gt;
        &lt;div class='text'&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>And setting the height of div <code>text</code> to 100% sets it to the height of <code>main</code> even though it's inside div <code>title</code>, then I think you did not specify the height of <code>.title</code> in your CSS.</p>

Q: Riverpod AsyncNotifier re-fetches API after logout and causes 401 A: <h2>Riverpod fix first</h2>
<p>The issue happens because <strong><code>ref.invalidate(profileDetailsProvider)</code></strong> disposes the provider, but if the <strong><code>AppBarWidget</code></strong> is still mounted/watching it (e.g., HomeScreen doesn't unmount fast enough after <strong><code>context.go(RouteNames.authScreen)</code></strong>), <strong><code>build()</code></strong> triggers again before navigation completes, causing the unwanted API call.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Option 1: Add <code>ref.mounted</code> check</strong> (Riverpod 3.0+): In <strong><code>build()</code></strong>, guard the API call.</li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>@override
Future&lt;ProfileDetails?&gt; build() async {
  state = const AsyncLoading();

  if (!ref.mounted) return null; // Abort if no longer mounted

  final repo = ref.read(profileRepoProvider);

  try {
    return await repo.fetchProfileDetails();
  } catch (e, s) {
    if (ref.mounted) { // Only set error if still mounted
      state = AsyncError(e, s);
    }
    return null;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><strong>Option 2: Watch auth state in <code>profileDetailsProvider</code></strong>: Make it depend on login state to auto-reset.</li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>final profileDetailsProvider = AsyncNotifierProvider.family&lt;ProfileDetailsNotifier, ProfileDetails?, bool&gt;(
  ProfileDetailsNotifier.new,
);

class ProfileDetailsNotifier extends AutoDisposeAsyncNotifier&lt;ProfileDetails?&gt; {
  @override
  Future&lt;ProfileDetails?&gt; build({required bool isLoggedIn}) async {
    if (!isLoggedIn) return null; // No fetch if logged out
    // ... rest of build()
  }
}

// Watch it in AppBar:
ref.watch(profileDetailsProvider(ref.watch(authStateProvider)))
</code></pre>
<p>Your router redirect + <strong><code>invalidate(authStateProvider)</code></strong> is already good for navigation; combine with Option 1 for safety.</p>
<h2>Better long-term: Try Flutter Event Component System Library</h2>
<p>For cleaner auth flows without these lifecycle headaches, consider <a href="https://github.com/FlameOfUdun/flutter_event_component_system" rel="nofollow noreferrer">flutter_event_component_system</a> (disclosure: I'm the author). It uses an Entity-Component-System pattern where state (components) is explicitly scoped to features, systems handle logic independently of UI, and logout can fully teardown auth state without providers rebuilding unexpectedly.</p>
<h2>User Auth Feature Example</h2>
<p>Here's a complete, self-contained <strong><code>UserAuthFeature</code></strong> for login/logout/profile:</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>// Components (typed state)
class AuthStateComponent extends ECSComponent&lt;AuthState&gt; {
  AuthStateComponent() : super(AuthState.unauthenticated);
}

class ProfileDetailsComponent extends ECSComponent&lt;ProfileDetails?&gt; {
  ProfileDetailsComponent() : super(null);
}

class LoginCredentialsComponent extends ECSComponent&lt;LoginCredentials&gt; {
  LoginCredentialsComponent() : super(LoginCredentials.empty());
}

// Events (triggers)
class LoginEvent extends ECSEvent {}

class LogoutEvent extends ECSEvent {}

// Reactive Systems (pure logic, no UI)
class LoginReactiveSystem extends ReactiveSystem {
  LoginReactiveSystem();

  @override
  Set&lt;Type&gt; get reactsTo =&gt; {LoginEvent};

  @override
  Set&lt;Type&gt; get interactsWith =&gt; {AuthStateComponent, LoginCredentialsComponent, ProfileDetailsComponent};

  @override
  Future&lt;void&gt; react() async {
    final creds = getEntity&lt;LoginCredentialsComponent&gt;();
    final auth = getEntity&lt;AuthStateComponent&gt;();
    final profile = getEntity&lt;ProfileDetailsComponent&gt;();

    try {
      auth.update(AuthState.loading);
      final tokens = await authRepo.login(creds.value.username, creds.value.password);
      await localStorage.setTokens(tokens.access, tokens.refresh);
      final details = await profileRepo.fetchProfileDetails(); // API call only when logged in
      profile.update(details);
      auth.update(AuthState.authenticated);
    } catch (e) {
      auth.update(AuthState.error(e.toString()));
    }
  }
}

class LogoutReactiveSystem extends ReactiveSystem {
  LogoutReactiveSystem();

  @override
  Set&lt;Type&gt; get reactsTo =&gt; {LogoutEvent};

  @override
  Set&lt;Type&gt; get interactsWith =&gt; {AuthStateComponent, ProfileDetailsComponent};

  @override
  Future&lt;void&gt; react() async {
    final auth = getEntity&lt;AuthStateComponent&gt;();
    final profile = getEntity&lt;ProfileDetailsComponent&gt;();

    try {
      await authRepo.logout();
      await localStorage.clearTokens();
      profile.update(null); // Explicitly clear profile (no API call)
      auth.update(AuthState.unauthenticated);
    } catch (e) {
      // Ignore logout errors
    }
  }
}

// Feature (groups everything)
class UserAuthFeature extends ECSFeature {
  UserAuthFeature() {
    addEntity(AuthStateComponent());
    addEntity(ProfileDetailsComponent());
    addEntity(LoginCredentialsComponent());
    addEntity(LoginEvent());
    addEntity(LogoutEvent());
    addSystem(LoginReactiveSystem());
    addSystem(LogoutReactiveSystem());
  }
}
</code></pre>
<h2>UI Integration (reactive, no manual invalidation)</h2>
<p>Wrap app: <strong><code>ECSScope(features: {UserAuthFeature()}, child: MaterialApp(...))</code></strong></p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>class HomeAppBar extends ECSWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context, ECSContext ecs) {
    final authState = ecs.watch&lt;AuthStateComponent&gt;();
    final profile = ecs.watch&lt;ProfileDetailsComponent&gt;();
    final logoutEvent = ecs.get&lt;LogoutEvent&gt;();

    return Row(
      children: [
        if (authState.value == AuthState.authenticated &amp;&amp; profile.value != null)
          Text('Welcome ${profile.value!.username}')
        else
          const Text('Guest'), // No API call here

        ElevatedButton(
          onPressed: authState.value == AuthState.authenticated ? logoutEvent.trigger : null,
          child: const Text('Logout'),
        ),
      ],
    );
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Why ECS avoids your issue:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>No global providers: State is feature-scoped; logout triggers <strong><code>LogoutReactiveSystem</code></strong> which clears <strong><code>ProfileDetailsComponent</code></strong> explicitly <strong>without</strong> refetching.</li>
<li>UI only rebuilds on watched component changes; no auto <strong><code>build()</code></strong> on invalidate.</li>
<li>Testable: Mock systems independently; no widget trees needed.</li>
<li>Scales: Features are modular (add auth without touching home).</li>
<li>Use inspector devtools extension for realtime visual debugging and more</li>
</ul>
<p>Install: <strong><code>flutter pub add flutter_event_component_system</code></strong></p>
<p>Cheers!</p>
